// Track A.9 — `map_bounded(items, n, f)` applies the async `f` to each item with at most `n` futures
// in flight, returning results in item order. The window SLIDES: with n=2 over five items, the next
// item starts the moment one finishes, so a slow item never holds a free slot idle waiting for its
// batch-mate. Results keep input order regardless of completion order.
//
// Each item sleeps `n * 20` ms, so the deadlines are 20 ms apart and the interleave above is a
// property of the program. Five equal sleeps come due at the same poll on a real host and resume in
// scheduler order, which also hides the difference between a sliding window and lock-step batches:
// under equal sleeps both produce start,start,end,end,start,start.
// expect: stdout "start 1"
// expect: stdout "start 2"
// expect: stdout "end 1"
// expect: stdout "start 3"
// expect: stdout "end 2"
// expect: stdout "start 4"
// expect: stdout "end 3"
// expect: stdout "start 5"
// expect: stdout "end 4"
// expect: stdout "end 5"
// expect: stdout "result=2,4,6,8,10"
// expect: exit 0
use std.task.{sleep, map_bounded}
async fn dbl(n: int): int {
    echo "start " ~ n
    sleep(n * 20).await
    echo "end " ~ n
    return n * 2
}
xs = map_bounded([1, 2, 3, 4, 5], 2, dbl)
echo "result=" ~ xs.join(",")
